              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Table 13.7"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table_body",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.101,
                0.163,
                0.896,
                0.443
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_8_table_3.jpg",
              "content": "<table><tbody><tr><td>Percentage of female teachers</td><td>Number of states/U.T.(f<sub>i</sub>)</td><td>x<sub>i</sub></td><td>d<sub>i</sub> = x<sub>i</sub>- 50</td><td><sub>ui</sub> =xi -5010</td><td>f<sub>i</sub>x<sub>i</sub></td><td>f<sub>i</sub>d<sub>i</sub></td><td>f<sub>i</sub>u<sub>i</sub></td></tr><tr><td>15 - 2525 - 3535 - 4545 - 5555 - 6565 - 7575 - 85</td><td>61174421</td><td>20304050607080</td><td>-30-20-100102030</td><td>-3-2-10123</td><td>12033028020024014080</td><td>-180-220-700404030</td><td>-18-22-70443</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>35</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>1390</td><td>-360</td><td>-36</td></tr></tbody></table>"
            },
            {
              "type": "table_footnote",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.147,
                0.456,
                0.695,
                0.479
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "From the table above, we obtain "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\Sigma f_{i} = 35"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": " , "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\Sigma f_{i}x_{i} = 1390,"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.395,
            0.484,
            0.678,
            0.507
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_8_equation_5.jpg",
          "content": "\\Sigma f_{i}d_{i}=-360,\\quad \\Sigma f_{i}u_{i}=-36."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.52,
            0.642,
            0.566
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Using the direct method, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\bar{x}=\\frac{\\Sigma f_{i}x_{i}}{\\Sigma f_{i}}=\\frac{1390}{35}=39.71"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.567,
            0.475,
            0.587
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Using the assumed mean method,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.322,
            0.6,
            0.702,
            0.645
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_8_equation_8.jpg",
          "content": "\\overline{x}=a+\\frac{\\Sigma f_{i}d_{i}}{\\Sigma f_{i}}=50+\\frac{(-360)}{35}=39.71"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.649,
            0.465,
            0.669
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Using the step-deviation method,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.32,
            0.681,
            0.818,
            0.729
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_8_equation_10.jpg",
          "content": "\\bar{x}=a+\\left(\\frac{\\Sigma f_{i}u_{i}}{\\Sigma f_{i}}\\right)\\times h=50+\\left(\\frac{-36}{35}\\right)\\times10=39.71"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.737,
            0.902,
            0.775
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Therefore, the mean percentage of female teachers in the primary schools of rural areas is 39.71."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.78,
            0.903,
            0.899
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Remark :",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " The result obtained by all the three methods is the same. So the choice of method to be used depends on the numerical values of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x _ { i }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "f _ { i ^ { * } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " If "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x _ { i }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "f _ { i }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " are sufficiently small, then the direct method is an appropriate choice. If "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x _ { i }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "f _ { i }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " are numerically large numbers, then we can go for the assumed mean method or step-deviation method. If the class sizes are unequal, and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x _ { i }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " are large numerically, we can still apply the step-deviation method by taking h to be a suitable divisor of all the "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "d _ { i } ^ { \\flat }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " s."
            }
          ]
        },
